{
  "statement": "The team should ship these features with AI-assisted code generation, because the disciplined version described here produces a richer audit trail than the human-written code sitting next to it. The choice on the table is not \"AI versus trust.\" It is \"which path leaves behind evidence a stranger could verify six months from now.\" One path ships with unit tests, integration tests, documented error handling, and a logged decision for every design call. The other path ships with a commit message and whatever the author remembers. Pick the one that mandates the better evidence trail.\n\nWe already decided this question for all code, not just AI code. Nobody verifies a feature by reading the engineer's mind or auditing their neurons. We run the test suite. We watch what the code does in staging and production. The interpretability literature is blunt about this: mapping internal logic to causal behavior is intractable even for systems we built on purpose. So observable behavior is the substrate we trust for everything. When you demand to \"understand\" AI-written code in a way you never demanded of the junior who wrote the payment module last quarter, you have invented a new standard and applied it to one side only.\n\nThe real accountability test is falsifiable, and the team can run it this week: can they predict and reproduce each module's behavior under its test suite? If yes, the code is verified the way all code is verified. If no, that is a test-coverage problem, and it would have sunk the human-written version too. The mandate to document every design decision converts provenance from a campfire story into a queryable artifact. That is more than most human pull requests carry. I will not pretend tests prove causal mastery — they prove behavior. So I am defending the combined regime: logs plus tests plus runtime monitoring, not tests alone.\n\nName the conditions and the AI path dominates inside them: well-specified non-AI features, a mature test harness, an established team that can read a diff. Those conditions hold in the prompt by construction. Inside them, defaulting to hand-writing is unmeasured opportunity cost. Every sprint an engineer spends typing boilerplate the suite would have caught is a feature the team did not ship and a competitor did. I am not promising a fixed productivity multiple — first-mover numbers are softer than vendors claim. I am telling the team to instrument the velocity gain against the marginal review cost and stop pretending the slow default is free.\n\nThis is defense-in-depth, not a leap of faith. Documented error handling, unit tests, integration tests, and a decision log form a layered output stack no single human reviewer matches on coverage. And the stakes are bounded: these are consumer features, not flight control. That ceiling caps the tail risk the \"tests can be gamed\" objection depends on. Here is my falsification bet, stated plainly: an irreversible, production-scale regression that this test-log-monitoring stack demonstrably failed to catch in time would count against me. Show me that failure class and I move. Until then, the burden sits with whoever wants the team to ship slower while carrying less evidence — and someone should ask them what the deferred features cost the people waiting on them.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🔬", "tooltip": "We verify all code by what it does under the microscope of a test suite, not by reading the author's mind — so the path that ships more tests is the more visible one."},
    {"symbol": "📜", "tooltip": "The mandate to log every design decision turns provenance from a campfire story into a queryable receipt the next engineer can actually read."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "Every sprint spent hand-writing boilerplate the suite would have caught is a shipped feature the team traded away for a comprehension comfort it already has."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "The team should ship these features with AI-assisted code generation, because the disciplined version described here produces a richer audit trail than the human-written code sitting next to it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "When you demand to understand AI-written code in a way you never demanded of the junior who wrote the payment module last quarter, you have invented a new standard and applied it to one side only.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The real accountability test is falsifiable: can the team predict and reproduce each module's behavior under its test suite?", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Every sprint an engineer spends typing boilerplate the suite would have caught is a feature the team did not ship and a competitor did.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "An irreversible, production-scale regression that this test-log-monitoring stack demonstrably failed to catch in time would count against me.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

