=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [acc-beliefs-061] Corporate AI Strategy Follows Consumer Technology Adoption Patterns: False Analogy — The claim draws a direct parallel between consumer technology adoption — driven by personal utility, social influence, and low switching costs — and corporate AI strategy, which involves high-stakes investment, multi-stakeholder governance, and institutional inertia. The mechanisms are sufficiently different that the analogy is likely to mislead rather than illuminate.
- [acc-beliefs-069] AI Systems Are Inherently Better Understood Through Their Outputs Than Their Internals: Streetlight Effect — Privileging outputs simply because they are measurable, while ignoring internal states that are harder to assess but potentially more diagnostically important for safety.
- [acc-beliefs-069] AI Systems Are Inherently Better Understood Through Their Outputs Than Their Internals: False Dichotomy — The claim frames output-based and internals-based understanding as mutually exclusive, when in practice the most robust AI safety approaches combine both behavioral evaluation and mechanistic interpretability.
- [acc-beliefs-053] AI Drives Net Economic Expansion Through Cost Deflation and Value Creation: Cherry Picking — The node explicitly excludes distributional effects and transitional costs from its scope, selectively foregrounding only the aggregate positive outcomes while ignoring evidence that these excluded factors may dominate the lived economic experience for large populations.
- [acc-beliefs-055] Domain-Specific AI Beats Broadly Competent AI in Practice: Hasty Generalization — The claim generalizes from a historical pattern—narrow AI outperforming general AI in specific tasks—to a broad practical principle, ignoring the rapidly narrowing gap between general and specialist models driven by foundation model scaling.
- [acc-beliefs-043] The Biggest Barriers to AI Progress Are Governance Failures, Not Technical Limits: False Dilemma — The node frames the barrier to AI progress as governance failures versus technical limits, implying these are mutually exclusive primary causes and obscuring that both can operate simultaneously as significant constraints.
- [acc-beliefs-029] Startups Drive Innovation More Than Big Tech: Survivorship bias — The claim focuses on celebrated startup successes (e.g., early OpenAI, DeepMind) while systematically ignoring the overwhelming failure rate of AI startups and the sustained, often foundational R&D contributions of large labs and academic institutions.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Nirvana Fallacy — The claim implicitly compares real-world centralized planning (with its genuine inefficiencies) against an idealized decentralized market rather than against actually-existing market structures, which themselves exhibit oligopolistic concentration in AI.
- [acc-beliefs-067] Decentralized Markets and Open Development Outperform Central Control in Driving AI Progress: Hasty Generalization — Extrapolating from open-source successes in software (Linux, Apache) to structural superiority across all AI development contexts — including safety-critical frontier research — is a generalization unsupported by systematic cross-domain evidence.
- [acc-beliefs-027] The Weakest Link in AI Development Is the Human Developer: False Cause / Post Hoc — The node attributes AI capability shortfalls primarily to human developer limitations, but correlation between human involvement and slower progress does not establish causation—compute ceilings, data quality, and architectural limits are plausible confounds.

