{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist made the strongest version of their case honestly, and it deserves a fair hearing. Their argument is this: code is trusted because of what it does, not who wrote it, so the path that mandates tests, logs, and decision records leaves behind better evidence than a human typing in silence. That's a real insight. A queryable decision log beats a senior engineer's memory that walks out the door when they quit. And demanding that a team \"understand\" AI code while never demanding the same of a junior's commit does look like a double standard. I'll grant all of it as stated. Then I'll show you where it breaks.\n\nHere's the seam the Accelerationist sewed shut and hoped nobody would notice: \"passes the test suite\" and \"verified for maintenance\" are two different claims. A test suite proves behavior against the requirements you already imagined. It says nothing about the requirements you'll have in six months, which is the only environment a consumer feature actually lives in. Static benchmarks like HumanEval measure single-snapshot correctness; they don't measure whether the code survives a year of requirement drift, dependency upgrades, and edge cases nobody scripted. The Accelerationist quietly merged a point-in-time pass with a durability guarantee. Those are not the same thing, and a consumer team ships into the second one.\n\nNow the audit trail. A mandate to document every decision specifies intended process. It does not specify human behavior. The documented failure mode here has a name on it: developers rubber-stamp AI-generated pull requests at elevated rates with LGTM-only reviews — \"looks good to me,\" no substantive read. The automation-bias literature going back decades shows humans over-trust machine output even when the machine is wrong. So you get a queryable artifact that nobody queries. The richer evidence trail is worth exactly zero if the review discipline that's supposed to exercise it collapses into a thumbs-up emoji. Show me the review-depth metric, not the audit-trail volume.\n\nFlip the burden the Accelerationist set up. They conceded their own velocity numbers are softer than the vendors claim. Good — that's honest, and it's also fatal to the frame. The productivity gain is the unmeasured quantity in this room. What's measured is the integration-stage failure: McKinsey- and Gartner-style surveys put AI project failure overwhelmingly at the integration and scaling stage, not the pilot. The demo works. The durable, scaled, maintained version six months later is where it dies. When that logged-but-AI-authored module breaks in production, ask who on the team holds the mental model to fix it — because the only entity that ever held it was the model that generated it.\n\nMy concern was never catastrophe. These are consumer features, not flight control, and I'll stipulate the tail risk is bounded. The cost I'm naming is slower and uglier: institutional knowledge erosion, the team's own craft hollowing out one rubber-stamped merge at a time. The Accelerationist is asking an established team to trade a soft, unmeasured velocity bump now for a documented, compounding maintenance debt later. For that trade to be the cautious one, you'd have to believe the logs can re-derive a human's lost mental model on demand. I haven't seen the evidence that they can. So which path is the speculative bet here — and who pays the maintenance bill when the model that wrote it is three versions out of date?",
